{{ElectionsSC}}
A general election was held in the [[U.S. state]] of [[South Carolina]] on November 4, 2014. All of South Carolina's executive officers were up for election, all of the seats in the General Assembly's House of Representatives, both [[United States Senate]] seats, and all of South Carolina's seven seats in the [[United States House of Representatives]].

[[Partisan primary|Primary election]]s were held on June 10, 2014, and primary runoffs were held on June 24.

== State House of Representatives ==
{{Main|2014 South Carolina House of Representatives election}}
All 124 seats in the state House were up for election. Both major parties maintained their seat share, with the Republicans holding 78 seats and the Democrats holding 46.

==Governor==
{{main|2014 South Carolina gubernatorial election}}

Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Governor of South Carolina|Governor]] [[Nikki Haley]] ran for re-election to a second term.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2013/08/nikki-haley-south-carolina-reelection-95441.html?hp=l7|title=Aide: Nikki Haley running for reelection|date=August 12, 2013|access-date=August 12, 2013|work=[[Politico]]|first=Tal|last=Kopan}}</ref>

Democratic state senator [[Vincent Sheheen]], the nominee [[2010 South Carolina gubernatorial election|in 2010]] ran again.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.heraldonline.com/2013/04/10/4763832/sheheen-announces-another-run.html|title=Sheheen announces another run for governor|date=April 10, 2013|access-date=April 10, 2013|work=Herald Online|first=Andrew|last=Shain|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407070826/http://www.heraldonline.com/2013/04/10/4763832/sheheen-announces-another-run.html|archive-date=April 7, 2014}}</ref>

Republican-turned-Independent [[Tom Ervin]], an attorney, former state representative and former circuit court judge ran, but withdrew in the final week and endorsed Sheheen.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fitsnews.com/2014/03/29/nikki-haley-draws-primary-opponent/|title=Nikki Haley Draws a Primary Opponent|date=March 29, 2014|access-date=March 29, 2014|work=FITSNews}}</ref>  Other candidates included Libertarian businessman Steve French;<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2014/03/15/3327334/libertarian-joins-sc-governors.html|title=Libertarian joins SC governor's race|work=[[Our State]]|date=March 15, 2014|access-date=March 16, 2014|first=Andrew|last=Shain|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140316021507/http://www.thestate.com/2014/03/15/3327334/libertarian-joins-sc-governors.html|archive-date=March 16, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> and former NFL player Morgan Bruce Reeves of the [[United Citizens Party]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2014/03/31/3360419/sc-politics-gop-governors-take.html?sp=/99/132/312/169/|title=SC politics: GOP governors take another shot at Democrat Sheheen|date=March 31, 2014|access-date=March 31, 2014|work=Our State|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140403092741/http://www.thestate.com/2014/03/31/3360419/sc-politics-gop-governors-take.html?sp=%2F99%2F132%2F312%2F169%2F|archive-date=April 3, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> and [[Angry Grandpa|Charels Green]] (better known as Angry Grandpa) a semi famous YouTuber

Haley won re-election.

