==United States Senate==
'''Regularly scheduled election'''<br />
{{main|United States Senate election in South Carolina, 2014}}
Incumbent Republican senator [[Lindsey Graham]] is running for re-election to a third term in office.<ref name=thestate>{{cite web |last=Rosen |first=James |url=http://www.thestate.com/2012/02/19/2159087/graham-i-expect-a-primary-challenge.html#.ULDuhYfoSIw |title=WASHINGTON: Graham: 'I expect' a primary challenge &#124; News |publisher=Our State |access-date=February 14, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140221123003/http://www.thestate.com/2012/02/19/2159087/graham-i-expect-a-primary-challenge.html#.ULDuhYfoSIw |archive-date=February 21, 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He faced six challengers in the Republican primary: pastor and businessman Det Bowers,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2014/02/04/pastor-joins-crowded-gop-race-to-unseat-lindsey-graham/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140204210850/http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2014/02/04/pastor-joins-crowded-gop-race-to-unseat-lindsey-graham/|url-status=dead|archive-date=February 4, 2014|title=Pastor joins crowded GOP race to unseat Lindsey Graham|date=February 4, 2014|access-date=February 4, 2014|work=[[CNN]]|last=Hamby|first=Peter}}</ref> State Senator [[Lee Bright]],<ref name=MaceBright>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2013/08/01/2894539/mace-bright-expected-to-face-graham.html|title=First Citadel female grad, Sen. Bright will take on Graham|date=August 1, 2013|access-date=August 2, 2013|work=Our State|first=Andrew|last=Shain|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130804061001/http://www.thestate.com/2013/08/01/2894539/mace-bright-expected-to-face-graham.html|archive-date=August 4, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> businessman and candidate for [[South Carolina's 3rd congressional district]] in [[United States House of Representatives elections in South Carolina, 2010#District 3|2010]] Richard Cash,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.greenvilleonline.com/viewart/20130416/NEWS/304160068/Republican-Cash-run-against-Graham-2014|title=Republican Cash to run against Graham in 2014|date=April 16, 2013|access-date=April 16, 2013|agency=Associated Press|publisher=[[The Greenville News]]}}{{dead link|date=May 2023|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> attorney, [[Lieutenant colonel (United States)|Lieutenant Colonel]] in the [[United States Army Reserve]] and candidate for [[Lieutenant Governor of South Carolina|lieutenant governor]] in [[2010 South Carolina elections|2010]] Bill Connor,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2013/11/08/3085217/bill-connor-becomes-lindsey-grahams.html|title=SC Senate race: Bill Connor becomes Lindsey Graham's 4th GOP opponent|date=November 8, 2013|access-date=November 10, 2013|work=[[Our State]]|last=Self|first=Jamie|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131110030416/http://www.thestate.com/2013/11/08/3085217/bill-connor-becomes-lindsey-grahams.html|archive-date=November 10, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> attorney Benjamin Dunn<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2014/03/13/3324965/despite-ambiguously-gay-barb-four.html|title=Despite 'ambiguously gay' barb, four Graham foes unite|date=March 13, 2014|access-date=March 18, 2014|work=[[Our State]]|last=Self|first=Jamie|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140317105503/http://www.thestate.com/2014/03/13/3324965/despite-ambiguously-gay-barb-four.html|archive-date=March 17, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> and businesswoman and author [[Nancy Mace]].<ref name=MaceBright/> Graham won the primary with 56% of the vote, negating the need for a runoff.

State Senator [[Brad Hutto]] defeated entrepreneur [[Jay Stamper]] in the Democratic primary.<ref name="WLTX">{{cite web|title= Brad Hutto wins democratic primary |url=http://www.wltx.com/story/news/local/2014/06/10/brad-hutto-wins-democratic-primary/10306321/|publisher=WTLX|date=June 10, 2014|access-date=February 12, 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://archive.today/20150212135102/http://www.wltx.com/story/news/local/2014/06/10/brad-hutto-wins-democratic-primary/10306321/|archive-date=February 12, 2015}}</ref>

Former Republican state treasurer [[Thomas Ravenel]] is running as an independent.<ref name=charm>{{cite web|author=Schuyler Kropf|url=http://www.thestate.com/2014/04/08/3374664/southern-charm-recap-thomas-ravenel.html|title=Southern Charm recap: Thomas Ravenel and the U.S. Senate?|publisher=The State|date=April 8, 2014|access-date=April 8, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140408223518/http://www.thestate.com/2014/04/08/3374664/southern-charm-recap-thomas-ravenel.html|archive-date=April 8, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> Libertarian Victor Kocher<ref name=whosfiled/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://thetandd.com/news/hutto-seeking-u-s-senate-seat/article_2e0e788c-b6af-11e3-aa50-0019bb2963f4.html|title=Hutto seeking U.S. Senate seat|date=March 29, 2014|access-date=April 1, 2014|work=Fort Mill Times}}</ref> is also running.

'''Special election'''<br />
{{main|United States Senate special election in South Carolina, 2014}}
Incumbent Republican senator [[Tim Scott]], who was appointed to the office in January 2013 after [[Jim DeMint]] resigned, is running for election to the remaining part of the term. The seat will be up for election to a six-year term in 2016.

Scott defeated Randall Young in the Republican primary.<ref name=whosfiled/>

[[Richland County, South Carolina|Richland County]] Councilwoman [[Joyce Dickerson]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://northeastcolumbia.wistv.com/news/politics/76633-county-council-member-run-us-senate-seat|title=County council member to run for U.S. Senate seat|date=October 3, 2013|access-date=October 30, 2013|work=wistv|author=Renee Standera|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102025523/http://northeastcolumbia.wistv.com/news/politics/76633-county-council-member-run-us-senate-seat|archive-date=November 2, 2013}}</ref> defeated former [[York County, South Carolina|York County]] Councilman Sidney Moore<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.heraldonline.com/2014/03/27/5813780/former-york-county-council-member.html|title=Former York County Council member to run for Scott's U.S. Senate seat|work=[[The Herald (Rock Hill)|The Herald]]|last=Zou|first=Jie Jenny|date=March 27, 2014|access-date=March 27, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140328061555/http://www.heraldonline.com/2014/03/27/5813780/former-york-county-council-member.html|archive-date=March 28, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> and attorney and candidate for [[South Carolina's 7th congressional district]] in [[United States House of Representatives elections in South Carolina, 2012#District 7|2012]] Harry Pavilack<ref>{{cite news | title=Exclusive: Another unknown Democrat seeks US Senate seat in SC | author=Jamie Self | date=September 14, 2013 | publisher=Our State | url=http://www.thestate.com/2013/09/14/2980396/exclusive-another-unknown-democrat.html | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131101074218/http://www.thestate.com/2013/09/14/2980396/exclusive-another-unknown-democrat.html | archive-date=November 1, 2013 }}</ref> for the Democratic nomination.

Independents Brandon Armstrong, a painting contractor,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2013/11/27/3126271/election-2014-independent-collecting.html|title=ELECTION 2014: Independent collecting signatures to run against Tim Scott|date=November 27, 2013|access-date=February 5, 2014|work=[[Our State]]|first=Jamie|last=Sel|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140221114045/http://www.thestate.com/2013/11/27/3126271/election-2014-independent-collecting.html|archive-date=February 21, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> and Jill Bossi, former Vice President of the [[American Red Cross]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fortmilltimes.com/2014/03/24/3371394/tega-cay-exec-running-for-us-senate.html|title=Tega Cay exec running for U.S. Senate|date=March 24, 2014|access-date=March 26, 2014|work=Fort Mill Times}}</ref> are also running.

